C10ENE-AL+O2   =>  C4H6+NC5H10CHO-5+HO2        4.500E+13  0.00  3.910E+04      ! Saudi aramco mech 1.3 (no reference)
C10ENE-AL+OH   =>  C4H6+NC5H10CHO-5+H2O        4.76E+06   2.00 -1434.0         ! rate rule for secondary allylic h, heyberger et al., oxidation of small alkenes, ijck 34:666-677 2002. 
                                                                                    ! estimated uncertainty factor => 1.5
C10ENE-AL+H    =>  C4H6+NC5H10CHO-5+H2         7.5E+04   2.5   -1912.0         ! rate rule for secondary allylic h, heyberger et al., oxidation of small alkenes, ijck 34:666-677 2002.
C10ENE-AL+O    =>  C4H6+NC5H10CHO-5+OH         1.19E+11 0.70    3107.0         ! rate rule for secondary allylic h, heyberger et al., oxidation of small alkenes, ijck 34:666-677 2002). estimated uncertainty factor=1.5
C10ENE-AL+HO2  =>  C4H6+NC5H10CHO-5+H2O2       1.02E+04 2.50   10114.0         ! rate rule for seconary allylic h, scott and walker, addition of toluene, combustion and flame 129:365�377 (2002).  estimated based on ethylbenzene+ho2 
C10ENE-AL+CH3  =>  C4H6+NC5H10CHO-5+CH4        2.22E+00 3.50     4046.0        ! wjp: used primary rate rule from allylic h, heyberger et al., oxidation of small alkenes, ijck 34:666-677 2002). 
                                                                                    ! lowered ea by 0.65(bd primary allyllic - secondary allyllic). 0.65 is for ch3 abstractions reactions from dean and bozzelli combustion chemistry of nitrogen, 1997.  
                                                                                    ! estimated uncertainty factor=3
C10ENE-AL+CH3O2  =>  C4H6+NC5H10CHO-5+CH3O2H   1.02E+04 2.50    10114.0        ! use ho2 abstraction rate constant
!
C10ENE-AL+CH3O  =>   C4H6+NC5H10CHO-5+CH3OH     6.34E+10 0.00 2400.0  ! used secondary alkyl h rate.   reduce ea by difference between bd for seconary alkyl and secondary allylic with f=.2: 0.2(98.4-85.6)=.2*12.8=2.6  
                                                                           ! est. f is larger than oh which is 0.1.  reduced a factor by 2.6 for tying up rotor (used scott and walkers, c&f2002 a-factors to get 2.6)
! Abstraction of secondary CHs:
! assume 12 possible secondary C-Hs to abstract.
! secondary reaction rate rule from Sarathy et al. 2011, deg=12
! reaction is written in forward direction only since reaction is a compined process that included radical decomposition:
C10ENE-AL+O2    => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+HO2    1.2E14    0.00 49640.0
C10ENE-AL+OH    => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+H2O    2.81E+08  1.61   -35.0 
C10ENE-AL+H     => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+H2     7.80E6    2.40  4471.0 
C10ENE-AL+CH3   => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+CH4    9.06E0     3.46  5481.0 
C10ENE-AL+O     => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+OH     3.30E6    2.45  2830.0 
C10ENE-AL+HO2   => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+H2O2   3.78E2    3.37 13720.0
C10ENE-AL+CH3O2 => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+CH3O2H 6.12E1    3.58 14180.0
C10ENE-AL+CH3O  => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+CH3OH  8.70E11   0.0   4571.0 
C10ENE-AL+C2H3  => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+C2H4   2.4E12    0.0  16800.0
C10ENE-AL+C2H5  => C4H71-4+HEX5ENAL+C2H6   3.0E11    0.0  10400.0
!
! Decalin reactions
!
! High temperature reaction classes:
! Reaction Type 1: 1. Unimolecular fuel decomposition
! use analogy to MCH rate constants from Zhang, F., et al. Energy & Fuels  27(3):1679-1687 (2013). 
! Note one rotor is "tied up" upon ring openning in decalin.  This is not the case in MCH.
! This will likely lead to a 3-4 times higher A-factor compared to MCH. This is not taken into account in the rates below!
! Test rates by multiplying by 3.
! Breaks bond between C9 and C10 (bridge carbons):
!decalin=! High barrier ~120 kcal, Chae, K. and Violi, A., Journal of Organic Chemistry  72(9):3179-3185 (2007). => omit
!
!Breaks bond between C9 and C1:
!use MCH rate, multiply by 2 for degeneracy (4 C-C bonds can be broken rather than 2 in MCH)
! Assume the same fall-off as in MCH, but should be less due to more degrees of freedom in decalin
DECALIN=NBCH-1ENE                            2.76E+26    -2.74       94184        ! mch=c7h14-2, Zhang, F., et al. Energy & Fuels  27(3):1679-1687 (2013). 
